lesson k-lights-long-ago v=1 format=teach grade=0 unit=8 subject=history levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "Lights long ago"
  goal "Put lights in order from the oldest to the newest."

  look {
    scene 11x17 {
      candle c start=10 burnt=3 lit=1 jar=0 at=canvas(1, 1)
    }
    say """
        Long, long ago people had fires and then candles and oil lamps for light. A candle burns down
        as it gives light. Electric light bulbs came much later, about a hundred and fifty years ago,
        and now we light a room with a switch.
      """
  }
  do {
    level easy {
      worked k.older-light k=0 level=medium
    }
    practice k.older-light count=2 seed=2
    practice k.candle-burnt count=2 seed=3
    show k.light-without-power k=0 hard-k=1
    show k.switch-on-safely k=0 hard-k=1
    practice k.home-object-age count=2 seed=11 easy-count=1
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show k.dark-evenings
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show k.lights-in-order
  }
  remember {
    say "Fires, then candles and lamps, then electric lights."
  }

  grown-ups """
    Read each question aloud. Travel long ago put vehicles in order; this lesson is the step on from
    it, putting the lights of a home in order. Light a candle together (a grown-up lights it) in a
    dark room and see how little it shows. The electric light bulb was made to work by Swan and by
    Edison in 1879, as the chronicle's source gives; candles are far older (recalled). If the child
    is stuck, ask what we do when the power goes off. England, Reception, ELG Past and Present
    (recalled, not fetched): "Know some similarities and differences between things in the past and
    now, drawing on their experiences and what has been read in class".
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level the candle and the bulb are compared in the teacher's pen first, the candle has burnt a little, and the light without power is a candle."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the lights are an oil lamp and a torch, the candle has burnt far down, and the light without power is an oil lamp."
  }
}
